### 6. Permissions Configuration

Project permissions live in `.claude/settings.json`. It uses project-relative
patterns (`Read(**)`, not `/Users/you/...`), so it works on any machine and is
committed to git along with the rest of the project.

**Check it exists**:
```bash
ls -la .claude/settings.json
```

**If it's missing**, recreate it from the toolkit template:
```bash
cp .claude/knowledge/templates/settings.template.json .claude/settings.json
```

No editing required - there are no machine-specific paths to fill in.

**What it does**: pre-approves reading and writing files inside this project plus
a handful of safe shell commands, so you aren't prompted on every single step.
Anything genuinely risky (deleting files, force-pushing) still asks first.

**If you ever need a machine-specific override** (a local-only MCP path, an
experiment you don't want committed), put it in `.claude/settings.local.json`.
That file IS gitignored. Most workflows never need one.

